Title:
RustPack Intro (MSecOps) — Architecture, OPSEC Philosophy, and Use Cases

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post introduces RustPack, a commercial Rust-based packer/loader product aimed at penetration testers and red teams to generate evasive Windows payloads quickly. It focuses on reducing signature, emulation, and entropy-based detections via heavy polymorphism, anti-emulation logic, and custom low-entropy encodings layered over encrypted payloads. The tool emphasizes OPSEC guardrails to prevent accidental payload disclosure in cloud/sandbox analysis by enforcing environment checks (domain-joined/physical thresholds), external payload retrieval (file/registry/URL), and environment binding (domain/host), plus a configurable kill date. It also discusses bypassing userland hooks for loader primitives and optionally unhooking DLLs before payload execution, while warning that behavior-based detections still depend on the embedded payload (e.g., stock Cobalt Strike/Meterpreter/Rubeus). Supported inputs include shellcode, .NET assemblies, and native PEs; outputs include EXE/DLL, weaponized sideloading DLLs with export forwarding, CPL, Excel add-ins, PIC/shellcode output, service payloads, and randomized PowerShell. Additional features highlighted include an interactive PowerShell runspace (AMSI/ETW/CLM/AppLocker bypass claims) and “safe” remote injection techniques (threadless, PoolParty variants, Caro-Kann variant), with a warning system intended to reflect real EDR burn risk.

Technical Focus:
- Polymorphic code generation / randomized instruction insertion
- Anti-emulation techniques and sandbox evasion gating
- Low-entropy payload storage via custom encodings over encryption
- Userland hook bypass/unhooking; AMSI/ETW bypass options for .NET
- Payload staging/decoupling (disk file, registry, URL) + environment binding + kill date
- Process injection tradecraft (threadless injection, PoolParty-style, Caro-Kann variant)

Use Cases:
- Generate per-engagement Windows loaders that resist static signatures and basic emulation
- Stage shellcode/.NET/PE payloads without embedding plaintext artifacts in the loader
- Build DLL sideloading packages (export forwarding) for signed-host execution
- Produce alternative initial access formats (CPL, Excel add-in, PowerShell, PIC shellcode)
- Run interactive PowerShell in hardened environments (claimed AMSI/ETW/CLM/AppLocker bypass)
- Perform stealthier spawn-and-inject / remote process injection during post-exploitation

Keywords:
RustPack, packer, loader, Windows, red team, OPSEC, polymorphism, anti-emulation, entropy evasion, custom encoding, encryption, sandbox evasion, domain-joined check, environmental keying, kill date, userland hooks, unhooking, AMSI bypass, ETW bypass, ETWti, call stack detection, DLL sideloading, export forwarding, CPL, Excel add-in, PIC shellcode, PowerShell runspace, constrained language mode, AppLocker, process injection, ThreadlessInject, PoolParty, Caro-Kann, Cobalt Strike, Meterpreter, Rubeus, Sliver, Havoc, Brute Ratel, Nighthawk, nanodump